<ArticleLayout slug="benefits-of-extruded-soybean-for-livestock">
  <h2>In poultry feed</h2>
  <p>
    Extrusion is the best treatment for soybeans. It allows:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      better energy efficiency - an increase of 32% compared with raw
      beans, of 13% compared with toasted beans and of more than
      6% compared with infrared treatment,
    </li>
    <li>
      the complete destruction of the walls of the cells containing the oil,
      making it available to be attacked by the digestive enzymes,
    </li>
    <li>
      optimal preservation of the biological value of the proteins through dry
      extrusion, thanks to a gradual rise in temperature while the fall is
      instantaneous - the protein reaches 150&nbsp;°C for less than
      3&nbsp;seconds,
    </li>
    <li>
      the destruction of 90% of the antitrypsic factors, responsible for
      the poor digestibility of raw beans,
    </li>
    <li>
      no effect on the structure of the triglycerides: the maximum temperature
      of 150&nbsp;°C is below the decomposition temperature of the fat (more
      than 180&nbsp;°C), which makes them available to the animal. The bean
      contains in particular a useful level of omega&nbsp;6 (linoleic acid) -
      around 10% of the raw product - as well as of omega&nbsp;3
      (linolenic acid), particularly beneficial to laying hens,
    </li>
    <li>
      good preservation, thanks to a low moisture content and to the
      destruction of the lipolytic enzymes by extrusion.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>For dairy cows</h2>
  <p>
    Calculating the energy value provided by extruded soybeans in a standard
    way is complicated. The French system is the UFL (milk feed unit),
    commonly calculated using an equation based on the composition of the raw
    material. However, it takes into account neither the digestibility of the
    components nor the interactions between them. Another method of
    assessment must therefore be found. INRA developed the system of proteins
    truly digestible in the intestine (PDI), which consists in adding
    together:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the feed proteins that have passed through the rumen unchanged - the
      bypass proteins (PDIA),
    </li>
    <li>the proteins produced in the rumen by micro-organisms (PDIM).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Thanks to this method, it is possible to compare the characteristics of
    the extruded soybean and of the raw bean according to their residence time
    in the rumen. The longer the soybean stays in the rumen, the more the PDI
    content falls - but this is all the more true for the raw bean:
    −43%, against −16% for the extruded bean. Moreover, the
    extruded bean contains between 45 and 110% more PDI than the raw
    bean. As for bypass proteins, extrusion cooking increases their number by
    100 to 300%.
  </p>
  <p>
    These calculations demonstrate the profitability of extruding soybeans in
    dairy cow feed.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
